[Music]  foreign  this is your Tech news briefing for  Wednesday November 23rd I'm Zoe Thomas  for The Wall Street Journal  every year the Wall Street journal's  personal Tech Team tests out a range of  gadgets and gear and puts together a  list of their top picks for holiday  gifts but this year with the prices of  everything creeping up and layoffs being  announced left and right particularly in  the tech sector the team focused on  items that are sure to be worth their  money nothing that's destined for a spot  at the back of the closet covered in  dust  joining us to share their list is  personal Tech columnist Nicole Nguyen  personal Tech reporter Dalvin Brown and  personal Tech reporting assistant  Cordelia James  thank you all so much for coming on the  show hello hey thanks again all right  let's jump into this list I noticed  there were quite a lot of Fitness  related gifts on there Cordelia can you  tell us about one of them  yeah so one of the gifts that I  recommended was the dribble up smart  boxing gloves so it's super it's just  like a fun way to spice up your workout  from home routine So I myself am like a  boxing Noob I don't know too much about  just the best way to box and so this was  like an easy setup really just like a  good way to track your reps and there  are just so many different sessions that  you can follow both on demand and live  and if you ultimately don't want to  spend the 17 bucks a month for the  membership there are still a couple of  practice sessions available and at the  end of the day you still have a nice set  of gloves to use okay well you know in  that same vein there seem to be a number  of wearables on this list what did you  stick on and why Nicole so one of the  wearables that I chose on this list is  the Garmin epics it's a pretty chunky  watch like most garments are but it has  a touch screen and so it's the most  Smart Watch like wearable that I think  garmin's put out they typically have a  five button interface that requires DDR  Dance Dance Revolution style  choreography just to perform simple  tasks but the touchscreen makes it  really really easy and for people who  are interested in the outdoors or their  long distance athletes I think this  would be an amazing thing to open up on  Christmas day and I also chose the  Fitbit sense to wearable Fitbit hasn't  really come out with a smart watch in a  number of years the sense 2 was really  interesting though because it comes with  a stress sensor that can detect physical  indications of stress so when you're  stressed out the thing that you need is  your wrist to buzz and tell you that  you're stressed out and it'll nudge you  to take a little meditation or some  breathing exercises or a moment to relax  I think we can all probably use a nice  reminder to take a deep breath you know  over the last couple years we've  definitely been talking about more gifts  for the home since we've obviously been  spending a lot more time in our houses  Delvin are there any good gifts for the  home this year  yeah so one of the good gifts for home  that I recommended this year was the  echo dot with clock so it's another  Alexa enabled smart speaker but what  makes it really interesting is that you  can use it to extend your Wi-Fi network  and it's also useful because it has a  little digital screen that shows  temperature or song titles so if you  don't want to call out for Alexa as  often you don't have to because a little  screen will show it to you you know  Nicole this was more of a practical list  this year so what's a good practical  gift that you stuck on one of my picks  for this guide is a laptop sleeve by  moft which looks like a very boring  laptop sleeve from the outside but when  you take your laptop out and you fold  along these little folded lines it pops  up in this origami-esque way to become a  stand so for anyone who works from  coffee shops or is on the road a lot  this is the perfect thing I use it all  the time there are two height options  even a lower one for typing and then a  higher ones that you can get more  flattering angle when you're video  calling or just browsing the web what  about something fun Dalvin do we have  anything fun on the list  yeah so one of the fun items I  recommended this year is like a tick  tock Era music mixer and if you are a  content creator or a kid that likes to  make a lot of noise Ruckus FX is a great  great option and it's only 50 bucks I  have it right here  imagine downloading your favorite song  onto a little handheld device and then  like doing your favorite Tick Tock dance  but like each movement remixes the song  in a different way so like if this is  the song and you're like punching and  twisting  I'm not a tick tock dancer yeah it looks  like it's got this little handle it  looks almost like I don't know like a  bop it I'm trying to think of it too  um but like I think the idea is like you  can create really cool content dancing  to it in like you know a dark room and  just create something really visually  appealing and upload your remix track to  tick tock you know I think maybe if  we're looking for something that's a  little less ruckussy and a little more  hanging out and chilling I noticed that  there was this Samsung freestyle  projector it's 800 so it's more on the  expensive side of this year's list why  did you include it Delvin yeah so there  are a few things that make it somewhat  essential and certainly unique so the  projector has all the apps that you're  you may be used to seeing on a Samsung  Smart TV it comes with Netflix and all  that baked in but one of the things  that's really unique about it is you can  power it using a light socket so you  literally screw the projector in where  you would screw in a light bulb and you  can project mood lighting into a room or  you know if you put it above the dining  room table maybe it's a personalized  table setting for the holidays so you  know it does a lot more than just  project high quality images onto a white  screen it's pretty fun let's keep going  with this splurging trend if you want to  spend a little bit more for this holiday  season Cordelia anything you'd recommend  I'd recommend the valve steam deck it's  like this handheld gaming device for  those who love playing PC games the  graphics are absolutely amazing it's a  little bit bigger than something like a  Nintendo switch for example if someone  is a really big gamer in your family I  would suggest probably getting one of  the devices that have a lot more space  you can get one either 64 gigabytes 256  gigabytes or 512 that 5-12 one is about  a little over 600 like around 650  dollars but something else that you  should know is the games that take up  the most space and probably are like the  most detailed also drain the battery so  you might want to keep a backup battery  pack handy too I mean I think all Tech  gifts we should always think about the  batteries an important lesson for  everybody when they give Tech well  that's our personal Tech Team joining us  with this year's wsj tech gift guide  Cordelia James Nicole Nguyen Dalvin  Brown thank you guys so much for joining  us thank you thank you thanks for having  us  and that's it for today's Tech news  briefing heads up we'll be off tomorrow  for Thanksgiving but we'll be back with  more Tech news for you on Friday and in  the meantime you can find more Tech  stories on our website wsj.com  I'm Zoe Thomas for The Wall Street  Journal  thanks for listening  [Music]

